A||||7||RacinRob||Hints on replacing parts (Radiator, O2 sensor, Fuel filter)||||6||
Z||000000||RacinRob||05-16-2001||03:08 PM||||yes I did a search, maybe not a good one but it is a hard topic and all I found were reasons to change theses parts.  There might be more out there... I just didn't find it.<BR>I have two manuals on the cars and I have read them, but from experience I know that the jobs are hard than the steps they list so my question is "for people that have done theses repairs do you have any hints, or tricks that will help me. the repairs are Replacing the leaky Radiator with a used one, the O2 sensor, and the fuel filter(I think the one on there is original.)  <P>Specific Questions:<P>For the radiator do I need to remove the hood?<P>for the O2 sensor, the book says the O2 sensor needs special anti-seize coating on the threads, the one I bought has gray grease like stuff on the threads already, is that the stuff I need? it's a Bosch<P>For the fuel filter, do I have to relieve the pressure or can I just wrap a rag around it and pray that it doesn't spray all over me.  I did this with my cavalier. <P>Thanks for any info <P>PS it is an 85 gt<p>[This message has been edited by RacinRob (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000001||87GTZ34||05-16-2001||03:10 PM||||I can comment on the FF.  pull the fuse for it and start the car... of course the car won't start but it will hit the injectors w/ the pump not making pressure. I'm about to do the O2 sensor myself when the part arrives.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||05-16-2001||03:34 PM||||I must agree with 87GTZ34 - When you are replacing the filter, it's so easy to relieve the fuel pressure, there's no reason <B>not</B> to do it.  And, obviously, you really don't want to end up lying under the car with fuel around you.  Nor do you want fuel to squirt into your eyes.<P>When I last did my fuel filter, I relieved the fuel pressure, jacked and supported the car, and sprayed the fittings with Liquid Wrench.  Next I put a big (3lb ?) empty coffee can underneath the filter, to catch any fuel drips.<P>Used a fitting wrench (I think) plus another wrench to prevent the filter from rotating.  You don't want the filter to turn, so that it won't twist the metal fuel line on the other end of the filter.  If I am not mistaken, the fittings are SAE size, not metric.  <P>Once you have the lines loosened from the filter, you can remove the filter clamp using a 10mm socket.  Gently separate the fuel lines from the filter, and remove the filter.  <BR>As the manuals say, "installation procedure is (pretty much) the reverse of removal."<BR>Except you install the <B>new</B> filter  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Tighten things up, put fuel pump fuse back in, energize the pump by switching the key on and off a few times, and check for leaks.  Then, if all's well, start the engine and inspect carefully for leaks again.<P>Radiator?  O2 sensor?  Sorry, I don't know about those  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <BR>   ||||reg||4
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||04:06 PM||||I just did the O2 on my V6. unplug the connector first so you can use a box wrench on it. Dont get lazy and cut the wire, just in case you cant get it off easily.<P>  I would try to remove it once without using any WD40 or liquid wrench, cause if you spray that stuff all over and then get it on the new one, it will ruin it.<P> run the engine till the exhaust pipe is hot (just a few minutes) put the box wrench on it and presto, mine came off fairly easy, Id say about 50 ftlbs of torque and it let loose.<P>  make sure the grease on the threads of the new one cannot get on the sensor part of it, and carefully install it, make sure you dont touch the sensor part on anything down there, the threads or the side of the pipes.<P>  Plug the wire in and you are finished.<P>  I found out later its easier to reach the connector (on the V6 at least) from underneight the car, you can get two hands on it that way, but it can also be done if you take the square cover off the left side. would be easier if you took the engine lid off too. Its hard to get both hands down there to push the connector back together. If you have really strong thumbs you might be able to push it together with one hand.<P>  If you cant get the old one off, then you might want to think about soaking it in wd40 overnight, or heating the exhaust pipe with a torch (scary scary scary!).<P>  That grease stuff works surprizing well - mine came off with no problems.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||05-16-2001||04:26 PM||||O2 sensor, what fun! My burns on my wrist are still healing, along with the cuts from the heat shields. Ditto on having the car warm when taking it out. You do have a V6? Make sure you also take of the air intake pipe and bend the heatshield back to the firewall, otherwise you'll only be able to do about 1/16th of a turn at a time.  And hooking up the connector IS a beeyatch! ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||04:38 PM||||what heat sheild? I was able to get about a 120° swing on the box wrench, there wasnt anything in the way - sounds like I am missing some tin in there?<P>  what this web site needs is some really good jpgs all around the engine compartment that we can refer to and compair to what our cars look like - then we can just say "look at this jpg (post the url), the red thing in the lower left corner is...||||reg||1
